WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.150
Your app speaks English.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.010
Most of the world doesn't.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.270
Translating an app looks simple, until you spend a minute on the details.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.630
One seat, but three seats.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.490
Words that change their order.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.890
Prices and dates that every language writes its own way.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.910
And that's with just two languages.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.820
That's exactly why we built Palamedes.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.820
You just write your sentences, right where they're used.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.900
Your translators bring the languages,

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.720
and Palamedes takes care of everything in between.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:41.940
Here's how it works. That little Trans tag around your sentence is a macro:

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:46.300
it looks like plain JSX to you, and your build rewrites it later.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.890
One command lifts every sentence out of your code and

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:52.940
into catalog files your translators already know.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:57.460
They add the translations, and your code stays exactly as it is.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:01.950
Then your build does the clever part: every sentence becomes a short ID,

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.280
and every translation becomes ready-to-run code.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.040
The catalog is split into one piece per screen,

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:13.200
so each screen loads only its own words, in your visitor's language.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.160
And it doesn't stop there.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.680
Plurals, numbers and dates, server rendering and server actions,

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:28.400
a linter for your messages and an audit that catches every missing translation:

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:33.340
it's one complete toolchain, from the first sentence to the last translation.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:40.360
The wiring for your framework is already built, for Next.js, React Router, TanStack,

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:45.280
Solid, Waku and Vite, and every one of them is tested as a real app.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.830
And it's seriously fast.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.580
Its Rust core works through fifteen hundred files

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.550
in just seventy-three milliseconds.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.160
Working with a coding agent?

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.780
Add the Palamedes skill and simply ask it to set up translations for you.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.440
Clear, complete, and fast.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:07.720
For once, you get to pick all three.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.040
Palamedes is open source.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.760
Get started today at palamedes.dev.
